in @prism
in @meta
in @glass
in @nl
in @kintsugi/consent
in @kintsugi/morphism
in @epistemologic/pact/gate_matches_diff_closure

# @kintsugi/fracture/gate — the kintsugi fracture body that resolves a
# pre-commit gate's check set to its diff-closure intersection. When
# the gate runs a check whose dependency closure does NOT intersect
# the commit diff (over-running) or skips a check whose closure DOES
# intersect (under-running), this body emits the morphism that swaps
# the gate's run set to the substrate-derived diff-closure intersection.
#
# THE OPERATIONAL HALF of bilateral pattern #53's SECOND INSTANCE.
# The declarative half landed at
# `@epistemologic/pact/gate_matches_diff_closure` (sibling tick);
# this shard names the auto-fracture the kintsugi loop applies when
# the property's transparency surfaces a gate/closure opacity.
#
# The friction this lifts to substrate: across this session, the
# pre-commit hook ran `cargo test` even on commits whose diff touched
# zero `.rs` files (pure-markdown, pure-.mirror commits). Reed used
# `--no-verify` as workaround. The substrate-correct behaviour: skip
# checks whose dependency closure does not intersect the diff. This
# fracture body QUOTES the corrected gate at AST altitude via
# splinter(ast); the byte-level hook rewrite is a separate operational
# tick under Taut's territory (@io/cargo, @mirror/spec, Justfile).
# The substrate names the proposal; @io applies it.
#
# Load-bearing context: recognition #43 (mirror IS a content-addressed
# build system; candidate — surfaced 2026-06-10); recognition #51
# (mirror as expanding Hilbert space; promoted) — fracture bodies are
# how the substrate maintains coherence by resolving altitude-level
# distinctions at each Bateson level. The gate altitude is the
# pre-commit altitude; the diff-closure intersection is the check-set
# the substrate's content-addressed cache already names; this body
# restores tracking when the gate drifts.
#
# === The bilateral pattern (candidate recognition #53, second instance) ===
#
# `@epistemologic/property/<predicate>` declares WHAT must hold; a
# matching `@kintsugi/fracture/<predicate>` declares HOW to restore
# the property when it fails. First instance: keyword/depth
# (@epistemologic/pact/keyword_matches_depth +
# @kintsugi/fracture/keyword). Second instance: gate/diff-closure
# (this shard + the sibling property).
#
# Two instances under one shape lift candidate #53 from candidate to
# recognised. The pattern's signature: declarative property emits
# `transparency` with located `opacity_map`; fracture body reads one
# opacity, emits one `morphism` whose `content` is a `splinter(ast)`
# at the corrected altitude; kintsugi loop's `active_pass` composes
# the per-pulse candidate set from the failure verdict.
#
# === Substrate-pull-correct home ===
#
# Per the form/process partition (recognition #55 candidate; landed
# 2026-06-10 at mirror `20eaf15`): @kintsugi is the process-side
# family root. Fracture bodies emit morphisms the kintsugi loop
# applies — process-side by structure. Home: @kintsugi/fracture/gate
# under the (forward-promised) @kintsugi/fracture family root.
#
# Substrate-pull alternative considered: @kintsugi/fracture/precommit
# (naming the host gate rather than the predicate it restores).
# Rejected: the bilateral pattern's signature is
# @kintsugi/fracture/<predicate> matching
# @epistemologic/property/<predicate>; the predicate is `gate_matches_
# diff_closure`; the shard species is `gate` (the noun in the
# predicate's subject position). Parallel to first-instance naming:
# property `keyword_matches_depth` -> fracture `keyword` (subject).
#
# === The fracture body ===
#
# Input: `opacity` (from `@glass`) — one located fact surfaced by
# `gate_matches_diff_closure`'s `failure(opacity_map)` verdict. Each
# opacity names ONE offending (check, commit) pairing: a check that
# is in the gate's run set but not in the diff closure (over-running)
# or in the closure but not in the run set (under-running). The
# `property` field carries the @nl description of which case applies.
#
# Output: `morphism` (from `@kintsugi/consent`) — the typed candidate
# the kintsugi loop's `active_pass` reads from this body and proposes
# through `query_phi`. The morphism's `content` ref names the rewritten
# gate (same path, run set replaced by diff-closure intersection); its
# `score` carries the dissonance reading (a gate/closure mismatch is a
# fixed, low-curvature dissonance — the rewrite is unambiguous once
# the closure is computed); its `expected` carries the cadence_kind
# the loop anticipates (authentic — the substrate settles cleanly once
# the gate tracks the closure).
#
# Spectrally: this fracture body IS one step along the Ricci flow at
# the gate altitude. The substrate's curvature at an over-running gate
# is the (run_set \ closure) excess; the negative-curvature direction
# IS the swap to (run_set ∩ closure); the resolved gate has zero
# curvature at this altitude (the closure fully determines the run set).
# Per `dissonance.mirror`'s `dissonance_as_holonomy`, the morphism's
# score IS the holonomy decrease the swap produces.
#
# Audibly: this fracture body IS the audible-altitude V → I cadence
# at the gate altitude. The pre-rewrite state holds the dominant
# tension (the wrong run set — false-positive friction); the
# post-rewrite state resolves to the tonic (the right run set); the
# cadence is authentic.
#
# === Substrate-pull discipline ===
#
# Per `@glass`, `opacity` is the substrate's located-fact carrier;
# this shard reads it directly. Per `@kintsugi/consent`, `morphism`
# is the substrate's typed-candidate carrier; this shard emits it
# directly. Per `@kintsugi/morphism`, `morphism_context` is the
# per-candidate slice that pairs a candidate with its pre-anchor;
# this shard's emitted morphism is consumed by the loop AND lifted
# into a morphism_context via `paired(oscillation.anchor, m)` at the
# active_pass altitude. Per `@epistemologic/property/gate_matches_
# diff_closure`, the consuming property's
# `gate_matches_diff_closure(gate, commit) -> transparency` is the
# gate that surfaces the opacity this body resolves.
#
# NO new verdict carrier; NO leading-`|` workarounds; NO opaque-`ref`
# carriers where structured types are available (per
# [[feedback-no-bare-types]]). The fracture body reads typed opacity,
# emits typed morphism — the substrate's vocabulary throughout.
#
# === Substrate gaps surfaced honestly ===
#
#   - `resolve_gate` reads one opacity at a time. The property's
#     `failure(opacity_map)` carries [opacity]; the kintsugi loop
#     iterates the map and calls `resolve_gate` per opacity.
#
#   - The morphism's `content: ref` carries the substrate handle to
#     the rewritten gate. The realisation layer produces the
#     rewritten splinter (same path, run set narrowed/widened to the
#     diff-closure intersection) and passes its handle as the
#     morphism's content. The substrate names the shape of the
#     proposal; the byte-level rewrite of the hook script / Justfile
#     recipe is @io's concern (Taut's territory) — NOT touched here.
#
#   - The `expected: cadence_kind` field is `authentic` for this
#     fracture body — the gate/closure rule is deterministic
#     (closure computation is content-addressed; run-set equality
#     is set-equality) and resolution always settles cleanly.
#
# === Pre-AI prior art ===
#
#   incremental rebuild  Karlton/Feldman (1976) make(1); the recompile-
#                        only-what-changed discipline. The substrate's
#                        reading is Erdweg/van der Storm/Völter (2014)
#                        on language-workbench correctness for
#                        incremental compilation.
#   dependency closure   Knuth (1968) TAOCP §2.2.3 on transitive
#                        reachability. The substrate's reading is
#                        the content-addressed cache (Nix; Eelco
#                        Dolstra 2006 thesis) — the closure IS the
#                        cache key.
#   gate restoration     Hoare (1969) Axiomatic basis; the predicate
#                        gate of a program command. The substrate's
#                        reading is Lawvere & Schanuel (1997) on the
#                        categorical pullback that restores
#                        commuting diagrams. This body's restoration
#                        IS the pullback that makes (gate, closure,
#                        diff) commute.
#
# The substrate's contribution is *typing* the per-site reduction so
# the kintsugi loop reads it. No invention.
#
# Spec context: recognition #43 (mirror IS a content-addressed build
#               system; candidate — surfaced 2026-06-10); recognition
#               #51 (mirror as expanding Hilbert space; promoted) —
#               the gate tracks the Bateson level at which the diff
#               lives; this fracture body restores tracking.
# Substrate decisions: [[architecture-shards-as-substrate-source]],
#                      [[architecture-prism-as-trait-as-everything]],
#                      [[architecture-glass-wall-substrate-types]]
#                      (opacity is substrate vocabulary; the fracture
#                      reads the typed carrier rather than threading
#                      an opaque ref through the loop),
#                      [[architecture-kintsugi-bias-lift]] (the
#                      kintsugi loop's bias direction is substrate-
#                      pull — gate/closure alignment IS one
#                      coordinate of the substrate-pull gradient;
#                      this fracture body fits the bias),
#                      [[architecture-kintsugi-variety-io]] (Ashby's
#                      requisite variety — the closure-computation
#                      partitions the @io check-space into
#                      diff-relevance-classes; the fracture body
#                      maintains the partition under mutation),
#                      [[architecture-mirror-as-content-addressed-build-system]]
#                      (mirror has every Bazel/Buck2/Nix primitive at
#                      substrate altitude; this fracture body names
#                      the partial-rebuild verdict's restoration at
#                      the pre-commit gate altitude),
#                      [[architecture-property-fracture-bilateral]]
#                      (the bilateral pattern this is the second
#                      instance of; promotion gate),
#                      [[architecture-splinter-ast-quote-primitive]]
#                      (the substrate's parametric AST-fragment
#                      construction; this body discharges via
#                      splinter(ast) declaratively, no Rust),
#                      [[feedback-no-bare-types]] (opacity and
#                      morphism are typed carriers; the body does
#                      NOT escape as a bare (check, commit) tuple).
# Forward-promise to consumers: `@kintsugi/oscillate`'s `active_pass`
#                  reads this fracture body when the candidate
#                  morphism_set is composed from property-surfaced
#                  opacities; the binding lands when the kintsugi
#                  loop substrate-pulls. This shard does NOT pull
#                  `@kintsugi/oscillate` or `@mirror/spectral/score`;
#                  the substrate-altitude arrow points inward (this
#                  fracture body IS consumed by the loop; not the
#                  other way around).
# Related shards: epistemologic/property/gate_matches_diff_closure.mirror
#                 (the declarative half of the bilateral; surfaces
#                 the opacities this body resolves),
#                 epistemologic/property/keyword_matches_depth.mirror
#                 + kintsugi/fracture/keyword.mirror (the FIRST
#                 INSTANCE of bilateral pattern #53; this shard +
#                 its sibling property are the SECOND INSTANCE that
#                 promotes the pattern),
#                 glass.mirror (the substrate floor; `opacity`,
#                 `location`, `transparency`, `verdict`, and
#                 `splinter(ast)` itself),
#                 kintsugi/consent.mirror (the morphism carrier
#                 this body emits; `query_phi` is the gate the
#                 emitted morphism flows through),
#                 kintsugi/morphism.mirror (the `morphism_context`
#                 carrier; the per-candidate slice the loop pairs
#                 this fracture's morphism with its pre-anchor),
#                 kintsugi/oscillate.mirror (the kintsugi loop
#                 driver; `active_pass` is the action that reads
#                 this body when composing the per-pulse candidate
#                 morphism_set).
# Out-of-scope (handed to @io / Taut's territory): @io/cargo.mirror,
#                 @mirror/mosaic.mirror, @mirror/spec.mirror, mirror.spec,
#                 Justfile, .husky/, .git/hooks/. The substrate names
#                 the typed proposal; @io applies the byte-level
#                 hook-script rewrite in a separate operational tick.
# Path-namespace property: this file at
# `shards/kintsugi/fracture/gate.mirror` declares
# `@kintsugi/fracture/gate` as a species under the (forward-promised)
# @kintsugi/fracture family.
# Keyword/depth property: this declaration is depth-2 from the
# @kintsugi family root; per recognition #46 it uses `glass`. The
# shard applies the first-instance bilateral's rule by birth.

glass @kintsugi/fracture/gate {
  focus gate
  project gate
  split gate
  shift gate
  settle gate
}

# === resolve_gate — the fracture body ===
#
# THE AUTO-FRACTURE: given an opacity site at a (gate, commit) pairing
# whose run set diverges from the diff closure, emit the morphism that
# rewrites the gate's run set to the diff-closure intersection. Same
# gate path, run set swapped.
#
# Reads an opacity (one element of
# `gate_matches_diff_closure(gate, commit).failure(opacity_map)`'s
# opacity_map); emits a candidate morphism the kintsugi loop proposes
# through `query_phi`. The morphism's three fields:
#
#   content   — substrate ref to the rewritten gate. The realisation
#                layer produces a splinter at the same gate path with
#                its `run_set` swapped for the diff-closure
#                intersection; the content ref names that splinter's
#                @mirror/store handle.
#   score     — dissonance reading. Gate/closure violations are
#                fixed-curvature dissonances; the score reads
#                `dissonance.is_pareto` cleanly (one strict winner
#                over the no-op alternative — the over-run or
#                under-run is strictly more dissonant than the
#                aligned run set).
#   expected  — cadence_kind anticipation. Always `authentic` for
#                this fracture body — the run-set swap fully resolves
#                the opacity at this altitude; the loop's `dark_pass`
#                will confirm identity preservation (the gate's
#                identity stays byte-equal at its path; only the
#                run_set field shifts); the loop's `read_consent`
#                bridges (pass, authentic) to the `settled`
#                oscillation_state for this opacity.
#
# The body is FULLY DECLARATIVE — substrate-pull-correct via
# `splinter(ast)` (declared at shards/glass.mirror's tick-2 carrier).
# The morphism's `content` field carries a content-addressed splinter
# at the @meta/ast altitude: same gate path, run set rewritten to the
# closure intersection. The substrate's compiler verifies the
# construction is deterministic and well-typed; the kintsugi loop's
# `active_pass` reads the splinter as the morphism's proposed content
# and applies it through `query_phi`.
#
# Per `splinter(ast)`'s parametric specialisation (glass.mirror
# §splinter(ast), 2026-06-10): the record IS its content-addressed
# handle — `content: oid` and `oid = ref` (shards/mirror/store.mirror
# line ~104) collapse the splinter literal to a substrate-pull-correct
# `ref` in the morphism's `content` field position. No Rust AST
# manipulation; no `\` obligation; no hook-script edit — the
# byte-level rewrite is @io's tick.
#
# Field-by-field substrate-pull reading:
#
#   content.content     — `opacity.location.file` (the substrate's
#                          located-fact ref at the source gate); the
#                          rewritten AST is content-addressed at the
#                          same path.
#   content.ast         — `@meta/ast` (the canonical altitude ref);
#                          per `boot/01-meta.mirror`'s `type ast(g)`.
#                          The run-set swap happens at the AST
#                          altitude by construction.
#   content.transparency — `success` (per `transparency(p)`'s success
#                          variant in glass.mirror): the run-set swap
#                          fully resolves the opacity at this altitude;
#                          no residual transparency remains.
#   score.roughness     — `opacity.property` (the @nl description of
#                          whether the violation was over-running or
#                          under-running, plus which check name); the
#                          substrate's `dissonance` reads the per-site
#                          roughness via the property text per
#                          dissonance.mirror's Helmholtz/Plomp-Levelt
#                          carrier shape.
#   score.partials      — `1` (the gate's run set IS the single partial
#                          of dissonance at this altitude; the swap
#                          collapses one mismatch).
#   expected            — `authentic` (the V → I cadence at the gate
#                          altitude; per cadence.mirror's `cadence_kind`
#                          and the loop's `read_consent` bridge:
#                          authentic → pass → auto-apply once @io
#                          binds).
resolve_gate(opacity: opacity) -> morphism {
  morphism {
    content: splinter(ast) {
      content:      opacity.location.file,
      ast:          @meta/ast,
      transparency: success,
    },
    score: dissonance {
      roughness: opacity.property,
      partials:  1,
    },
    expected: authentic,
  }
}

out @kintsugi/fracture/gate
out resolve_gate
